WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:02.250
Instructor: Okay, so to make an annotated tag,

00:02.250 --> 00:05.442
we use the dash A option for annotated,

00:05.442 --> 00:07.470
otherwise it's still git tag

00:07.470 --> 00:09.900
and then some tag that we're creating.

00:09.900 --> 00:11.400
And just like before

00:11.400 --> 00:14.700
it will be based upon the current head commit.

00:14.700 --> 00:16.470
Let's do some more significant changes.

00:16.470 --> 00:18.780
Now let's go make a new file

00:18.780 --> 00:22.320
and we'll just call this new feature.js.

00:22.320 --> 00:23.400
And then I'll put some stuff

00:23.400 --> 00:26.590
in here that says this is a new feature

00:27.510 --> 00:29.493
not breaking change.

00:30.570 --> 00:32.370
And then we'll put some gibberish here.

00:32.370 --> 00:34.590
We'll pretend that this is, you know, some nice new feature

00:34.590 --> 00:35.550
but you don't have to use it.

00:35.550 --> 00:37.890
It's just gonna be part of a minor release.

00:37.890 --> 00:40.143
Okay, so now I'm gonna add and commit.

00:41.040 --> 00:46.040
So git add new feature, git commit, add, new feature.

00:46.260 --> 00:48.510
Great and now I wanna create my tag.

00:48.510 --> 00:51.360
So git tag and then I'll do an annotated tag.

00:51.360 --> 00:53.280
So dash a.

00:53.280 --> 00:55.830
And then after that, the name

00:55.830 --> 00:59.940
of the tag that I wanna create, I think we're on 17 points.

00:59.940 --> 01:03.930
If we're doing a a minor release, it'll be 1.0, right?

01:03.930 --> 01:08.310
We were on oh 0.3 I believe 17.0 0.3.

01:08.310 --> 01:10.410
This is now a minor release, not a patch.

01:10.410 --> 01:13.590
So there we are and you'll see that it's gonna prompt me

01:13.590 --> 01:16.083
in my editor, just like when I do a commit.

01:16.980 --> 01:19.080
So it, you know, it wants me to include a message

01:19.080 --> 01:22.590
additional details if I want to, I can put stuff

01:22.590 --> 01:27.070
in here like this is more stuff about my tag

01:28.500 --> 01:32.250
it's an annotated tag.

01:32.250 --> 01:33.960
Sure, all right.

01:33.960 --> 01:38.160
And then I close out of here and that finishes the process.

01:38.160 --> 01:41.670
I now have that new tag and we see it down here.

01:41.670 --> 01:44.160
That is my new minor release.

01:44.160 --> 01:46.500
So that's how we make an annotated tag.

01:46.500 --> 01:48.240
Just like with Git Commit

01:48.240 --> 01:50.730
you can actually use the dash M option to do it

01:50.730 --> 01:52.980
in line to pass a message directly.

01:52.980 --> 01:55.560
You can forego the opening of the text editor

01:55.560 --> 01:56.790
just like git Commit.

01:56.790 --> 01:58.230
You still have to pass that in though.

01:58.230 --> 02:02.100
So if you use dash M, pass in a message between quotes.

02:02.100 --> 02:05.590
So how do we view more information about one of those tags

02:06.680 --> 02:08.010
right we just made this annotated tag.

02:08.010 --> 02:09.810
How do I see who made it?

02:09.810 --> 02:12.540
How do I see that metadata, the tag message?

02:12.540 --> 02:16.680
Well we use a command called git show and then the name

02:16.680 --> 02:17.513
of the tag.

02:17.513 --> 02:22.513
So 17.1.0 this is going to show us the tagger.

02:22.830 --> 02:25.320
There I am Colt, the date.

02:25.320 --> 02:27.330
Here's my tag information, right?

02:27.330 --> 02:30.390
This is the message I wrote and then the commit

02:30.390 --> 02:31.830
that it refers to.

02:31.830 --> 02:33.300
So you can see that right there.

02:33.300 --> 02:36.690
So we can also do this for real tags that others

02:36.690 --> 02:37.620
have created.

02:37.620 --> 02:42.620
Like let's look at 17.0.0 that major release.

02:42.780 --> 02:47.550
So we can do git show 17.0.0.

02:47.550 --> 02:50.130
So here's the commit that the tag refers to.

02:50.130 --> 02:52.890
We can see yeah bump versions for 17.

02:52.890 --> 02:56.820
Dan Abramov, very well relatively famous developer

02:56.820 --> 02:57.653
on the React team.

02:57.653 --> 03:00.120
Sort of the like React guy.

03:00.120 --> 03:02.040
And then we get a diff and you know

03:02.040 --> 03:05.550
this is exactly what happened in that one commit

03:05.550 --> 03:09.540
that this tag is referring to 17.0.0.

03:09.540 --> 03:11.430
So that's the basic concept

03:11.430 --> 03:13.980
of making an annotated tag dash A.

03:13.980 --> 03:15.840
It includes more information, it will prompt you

03:15.840 --> 03:17.880
in your text editor for a message

03:17.880 --> 03:21.390
or you can skip that and do dash M with the dash A

03:21.390 --> 03:24.360
at which point you need to pass in inside of quotes

03:24.360 --> 03:26.730
a message directly through the command line.

03:26.730 --> 03:29.190
And that will be used as your tagging message

03:29.190 --> 03:30.960
just like with Git Commit.

03:30.960 --> 03:33.210
And then we saw that we can use Git Show to

03:33.210 --> 03:35.643
see more information about a particular tag.
